<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    A tab, and it has to read as one rather than as a button or a link.

    What makes the difference is not the label, it is the join. A selected tab shares its
    background with the panel below it and has no border along the edge they meet, so the two
    read as one shape with a notch cut out of the top. An unselected tab keeps that bottom
    border and sits on a recessed background, so it reads as being behind the panel rather than
    beside it. Buttons all sit on the same plane as each other and links have no plane at all;
    this is the only detail that says "these are pages of the thing underneath".

    Built on RadioButton rather than on Buttons and a hand-rolled selected flag, because
    mutually-exclusive selection, keyboard arrows and the accessibility role all come with it.
    The template throws away the bullet entirely - what is left is the geometry above.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="D47.Tab" TargetType="RadioButton">
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Cascadia Mono,Consolas,monospace" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,7" />

    <!-- Negative bottom margin is what lands the tab on the panel's top border rather than
         above it. Without the overlap there is a hairline between them and the join reads as
         two separate controls that happen to be touching. -->
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,3,-1" />

    <Setter Property="Template">
      <ControlTemplate>
        <Border x:Name="Shape"
                Background="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}"
                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource D47.Border}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                CornerRadius="4,4,0,0">
          <Panel>
            <!-- The highlight on the current tab. A bar along the top edge rather than a
                 different fill, because the fill is already carrying the join to the panel
                 below and cannot say two things at once. -->
            <Border x:Name="Marker"
                    Height="3"
                    VerticalAlignment="Top"
                    Background="Transparent" />
            <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_ContentPresenter"
                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Panel>
        </Border>
      </ControlTemplate>
    </Setter>

    <Style Selector="^:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Text}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      The current tab: same fill as the panel, no border where they meet, an accent bar along
      the top, and the label at full strength rather than muted. The join alone says which page
      the panel is showing; the accent is what makes it findable at a glance, which is the whole
      job of a selected tab in a headset at a metre.
    -->
    <Style Selector="^:checked">
      <!--
        The label takes the accent as well as the bar. A 2px rule and a weight change was the
        first attempt and it was reported as no highlight at all, twice - correctly, because a
        thing you have to look for is not highlighted. This is the same idiom the settings
        sidebar already uses for its current section: accent bar, accent text.
      -->
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}" />
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#Shape">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Surface}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1,1,1,0" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#Marker">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}" />
      </Style>
    </Style>

  </ControlTheme>

  <!--
    The Settings tab, which is the same tab with one thing changed.
    <para/>
    The join is the whole idiom: a selected tab shares the fill of the page below it. The three
    transcript pages are D47.Surface and the settings page is D47.Background - it has to be,
    because its cards are Surface and a card the colour of its page is not a card - so a single
    checked fill cannot serve both. Everything else about a tab is inherited, so the two strips
    cannot drift apart in padding, weight, marker or hover.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="D47.Tab.Settings" TargetType="RadioButton" BasedOn="{StaticResource D47.Tab}">
    <Style Selector="^:checked">
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#Shape">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Background}" />
      </Style>
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>

  <!--
    A mode of the tab that is showing - Conversation, Technical, Log file; later Ships, Suits
    and weapons, Gap analysis (list.md Phase 25).

    Deliberately not a tab. "The mode control is not a second tab strip under the first; two
    stacked strips read worse than the eight flat tabs this avoids" - so this borrows nothing
    from the tab idiom above: no join to the page, no notch, no top marker. It is a segmented
    pill, which is the control every phone settings screen already taught, and it rides in the
    tab strip's own row rather than in a row of its own. Two strips is the thing being avoided,
    and a strip in the same row as the tabs is one strip.

    Shown at the root and nowhere else. Drilling hides it, because a mode switch three levels
    into a ship is a question about which stack you are in rather than about what you are
    looking at, and the breadcrumb is already answering the second one.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="D47.Segment" TargetType="RadioButton">
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Cascadia Mono,Consolas,monospace" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource D47.Type.Secondary}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.TextMuted}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,4" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />

    <Setter Property="Template">
      <ControlTemplate>
        <Border x:Name="Shape"
                Background="Transparent"
                CornerRadius="3">
          <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_ContentPresenter"
                            Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                            Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        </Border>
      </ControlTemplate>
    </Setter>

    <Style Selector="^:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Text}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Filled rather than underlined. The segment that is on has to be findable at a metre,
         and a fill is the one difference a glance reads before it reads anything else. -->
    <Style Selector="^:checked">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Background}" />
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Border#Shape">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}" />
      </Style>
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>

  <!--
    One crumb of the breadcrumb (list.md Phase 25, "Drill in, and find your way back").

    It is both the back affordance and the you-are-here, because a headset has no title bar to
    orient by and losing your place is expensive when you cannot glance at a second monitor.
    So every crumb but the last is pressable and the last one is not: the last crumb is where
    you are, and a control that does nothing is a control that costs a press to learn that.

    A button rather than a link, and quiet rather than accented, because the row is read as a
    trail rather than as a set of choices - the accent in this region belongs to the tab.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="D47.Crumb" TargetType="Button">
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Cascadia Mono,Consolas,monospace" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource D47.Type.Body}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4,2" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />

    <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource D47.SurfaceAlt}" />
      <Setter Property="TextBlock.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Accent}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Where you are. Disabled rather than drawn as a label, so the control keeps its metrics
         and the row does not shift by a pixel as the trail grows and shrinks. -->
    <Style Selector="^:disabled">
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Arrow" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource D47.Text}" />
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
      </Style>
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>

</ResourceDictionary>
